<section class="band">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head center">
      <span class="mos-eyebrow">Questions</span>
      <h2 class="mos-section-title">Honest answers</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq">
      <details open>
        <summary>Do I need to be technical? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p><strong style="color:var(--mos-color-text-strong)">Short answer:</strong> no — if you go with a cloud server.</p>
        <p><strong style="color:var(--mos-color-text-strong)">Longer answer:</strong> it depends on where MOS lives. A rented cloud server just means following our step-by-step guide, with video walkthroughs on the way — no experience needed.</p>
        <p>Your own hardware is more hands-on: it's like installing a Linux distribution, only simpler — flash a USB stick, boot from it, and wait. There are no prompts to answer; MOS sets everything up with sensible defaults. If you've ever installed an operating system, you're more than experienced enough. If not, a cloud server is the easier place to start.</p>
        <p>Either way, once MOS is running everything happens in the browser and works for anyone.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is it really free — what does it actually cost? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>MOS and every app in the catalog are free and open source — no license keys, no subscriptions, no paid tier.</p>
        <p>The only real cost is the computer it runs on: a little electricity if you use a machine at home, or a small monthly fee for a rented cloud server (often about the price of a couple of coffees). A one-click managed hosting option is coming for people who'd rather pay MOS to run it all for them.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where does my data actually live? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>On the machine you installed MOS on — your home mini-PC or your cloud server. MOS never routes your data through a MOS-owned cloud, and the platform contains no telemetry at all: no account, no analytics, nothing reporting back to us. We don't know that you installed it.</p>
        <p>The one exception is this marketing page, which counts anonymous visits using analytics we run ourselves. The docs have none, and your suite has none. The <a class="mos-link" href="/docs/privacy/">privacy policy</a> spells out every connection your server makes.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>How do my apps stay up to date? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>Each app updates on its own schedule, independently of MOS itself. Your server periodically checks a signed catalog for newer app versions and shows an <strong style="color:var(--mos-color-text-strong)">Update available</strong> badge — so a security fix for your password manager reaches you without waiting for a MOS release.</p>
        <p>Nothing updates behind your back. You see what changes — new settings, breaking changes, whether the privacy assessment moved — and you press the button. If an update fails, MOS puts the app back the way it was, or tells you exactly what it couldn't undo.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I install an app that isn't in the catalog? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>Yes. Paste a GitHub repository URL into the Apps search and MOS will fetch it, check it, and show you what it wants before anything is installed — which addresses it asks for, which storage, which other apps it wants to reach.</p>
        <p>It's labelled <strong style="color:var(--mos-color-text-strong)">External · Unverified</strong> everywhere it appears, because we haven't reviewed it and we won't pretend otherwise. Building it runs the publisher's own instructions on your server, so it's genuinely your call — MOS's job is to make sure it's an informed one.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What if I want to leave? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>Everything runs on standard open-source apps with standard data formats. Whole-suite backups go to storage you control — a USB drive at home, or a volume you attach to your server — so your data is always in your hands, and never hostage to a subscription.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>It's beta — is it safe to use? <span class="chev"><svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="#i-chevron"/></svg></span></summary>
        <p>MOS is version 0.x and improving quickly. Every change runs an automated test suite before it lands, the whole install-to-apps flow is exercised on real virtual machines, and every image is pinned to an exact digest. The changelog is public.</p>
        <p>Trust is the product, so here's the honest part: 0.x means breaking changes can happen, and we don't yet run the full end-to-end suite automatically on every release. Take a backup before you update — the button is right there.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
